===Pronoun===
{{en-pron|desc=singular or plural, nominative case|objective|[[whom]], [[who]]|possessive|whose}}

# {{lb|en|interrogative}} What person or people; which person or people; {{n-g|asks for the identity of someone; used in a direct or indirect question}}.
#: '''''Who''' is that?'' (direct question)
#: ''I don't know '''who''' it is.'' (indirect question)
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello Q1|act=I|scene=ii|page=8|passage=''Ia''[''go'']. Hee’s married. ''Caſ''[''ſio'']. To '''who'''? {{...}} ''Ia''[''go'']. Marry to. ---- Come Captaine, will you goe? ''Oth''[''ello'']. Ha, with '''who'''?}}
# {{lb|en|relative}} {{n-g|Introduces a relative clause having a human antecedent}}.
## {{n-g|With antecedent as subject.}}
##: ''That's the man '''who''' works at the newsagent.'' (defining)
##: ''My sister, '''who''' works in the accounts department, just got promoted to manager.'' (non-defining)
##*{{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title={{w|Mass Effect (video game)|Mass Effect}}|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2008|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]|system=PC|scene=Citadel|isbn=9780784546642|oclc=246633669|passage=Chorban: I don't really think my scanning disturbs them, but the authorities might disagree.<br>Chorban: I'd like to do it more openly, but it's not really worth getting arrested over.<br>Shepard: I could help you out. I'm not worried about the authorities.<br>Chorban: I don't even know '''who''' you are.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date= 3 March 2014|doi= 10.3390/rel5010219|author=Zoe Alderton|title= ‘Snapewives’ and ‘Snapeism’: A Fiction-Based Religion within the Harry Potter Fandom|work=Religions|publisher=MDPI|url= https://www.mdpi.com/2077-1444/5/1/219/htm |pages=219–257|volume=5|issue=1|passage=  Despite personal schisms and differences in spiritual experience, there is a very coherent theology of Snape shared between the wives. To examine this manifestation of religious fandom, I will first discuss the canon scepticism and anti-Rowling sentiment that helps to contextualise the wider belief in Snape as a character '''who''' extends beyond book and film.}}
## {{lb|en|non-formal}} {{n-g|With antecedent as object}}: [[whom]].
##: {{ux|en|That's the man '''who''' I saw earlier.}} (defining)
##: {{ux|en|My brother, '''who''' you met the other day, is coming to stay for the weekend.}} (non-defining)
# {{lb|en|fused relative|archaic|or|marginal}} [[whoever|Whoever]], he who, they who.
#: {{ux|en|'''Who''' insults my mother insults me.}}
#: {{ux|en|Give it to '''who''' deserves it.}} (marginal usage)
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|III|3|text='''Who''' steals my purse steals trash.}}

====Usage notes====
{{U:en:who and whom}}

* When “who” (or the other relative pronouns “[[that]]” and “[[which]]”) is used as the subject of a relative clause, the verb typically agrees with the antecedent of the pronoun. Thus “I who am...”, “He who is...”, “You who are...”, etc.

